New installation
================

1. Install a supported, patched PHP 8.4+ (8.x) runtime with PDO MySQL, sessions and
   filter support; MySQL 8.4/InnoDB is the deployment target. The source uses no
   Composer packages. PDO SQLite and Python 3.9+ are needed only for local tests.
   AXFR/query features also require operator-maintained tcpclient, axfr-get and
   dnsq binaries. The publishing host needs curl, flock, mktemp and tinydns-data.

2. Deploy into a new, read-only application directory. Only index.php and
   assets/app.css may be served. Configure Apache 2.4 to honor .htaccess or adapt
   deploy/nginx.conf. Deny src, bin, tests, deploy, .git, security-review, SQL files
   and all other source paths. Do not copy security-review into a release.
   Use a dedicated PHP-FPM pool/account; application code must not be writable by
   that account. Apply deploy/php.ini to the pool's runtime configuration.

3. Create a database using utf8mb4. Use a temporary migration identity with CREATE,
   ALTER, INDEX and CRUD privileges; the web identity needs SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE
   and DELETE only on this database. Do not grant FILE or global privileges.
   Set these environment variables in a private service/secret configuration,
   outside the document root. Ensure PHP-FPM explicitly receives them:

     VEGADNS_BASE_URL=https://dns.example.com/index.php
     VEGADNS_DSN=mysql:host=localhost;dbname=vegadns;charset=utf8mb4
     VEGADNS_DB_USER=<dedicated application identity>
     VEGADNS_DB_PASSWORD=<unique random database password>
     VEGADNS_SUPPORT_EMAIL=dns-support@example.com
     VEGADNS_EXPORT_TOKEN=<64 random hexadecimal characters>
     VEGADNS_SESSIONS=files
     VEGADNS_SESSION_DIR=/var/lib/vegadns/sessions
     VEGADNS_TRANSFER_DIR=/var/lib/vegadns/transfers
     VEGADNS_DNS_TOOLS=/usr/local/bin
     VEGADNS_DNS_SERVERS=192.0.2.53,192.0.2.54
     VEGADNS_TRUSTED_PROXIES=

   The sample IPs are documentation addresses; replace them with approved server
   IPs. An empty DNS_SERVERS disables outbound DNS operations. Restrict network
   egress to those addresses on UDP/TCP 53 as well. Generate the export token with
   `php -r 'echo bin2hex(random_bytes(32)), PHP_EOL;'` and store it securely. It is
   a service credential for all active zone data; never put it in URLs.
   TRUSTED_PROXIES may contain only actual reverse-proxy peer IPs. The proxy must
   overwrite X-Forwarded-Proto; the backend must be inaccessible directly.
   Cookie path is derived from BASE_URL. A subdirectory URL is supported when the
   webserver rules are adjusted to that prefix. All browser traffic requires HTTPS.

4. Create the session/transfer directories outside the document root, owned by the
   dedicated PHP account, mode 0700. File sessions need persistent local storage;
   select VEGADNS_SESSIONS=mysql for multiple application workers/hosts sharing DB
   sessions. Use the same export token and configuration across those workers.

5. With the migration identity in the environment, run:
     php bin/admin.php migrate
   Create a named administrator using a private password file (mode 0600):
     php bin/admin.php create-admin admin@example.com < /private/admin-password
   The password must contain 12-72 bytes. Use a password manager to generate it;
   do not put it in command-line arguments or shell history. Remove the private
   password file using your secret-handling procedure. Switch the service to the
   CRUD-only identity. No account is created by the web application or migration.

6. Configure PHP's mail transport and test mailbox delivery. Password reset sends
   a one-time link, valid for 30 minutes. It never sends or immediately changes a
   password. Exclude query strings from access/proxy/APM logs (reset links contain
   a short-lived token); never log cookies, Authorization or POST bodies. Run
   `php bin/admin.php cleanup` daily to prune expired security state. Sessions have
   an absolute one-hour login lifetime and are revoked on credential/role changes.

7. Sign in and configure system SOA/NS defaults before adding production domains.
   DOMAIN is a case-insensitive whole-label placeholder in default host/value
   fields; it is not replaced inside a label such as mydomain. Group defaults,
   when present, replace system defaults. Every active zone needs exactly one SOA.
   Domains created by users/group admins start inactive; a senior admin activates
   them. Automatic PTR records require an active reverse zone with the same owner
   and group as the forward zone. Otherwise manage PTR in its authorized zone.

8. On the publishing host, store a curl config outside the webroot, mode 0600:
     header = "Authorization: Bearer <same export token>"
   Do not enable insecure TLS or redirects in that file. Set VEGADNS_URL to the
   canonical HTTPS index.php URL, VEGADNS_CURL_CONFIG to that file, TINYDNS_ROOT to
   the existing tinydns root, and TINYDNS_DATA to the absolute compiler path.
   Run `sh update-data.sh` from cron under the account owning the tinydns root.
   The script locks publication, downloads over verified HTTPS, rejects HTTP
   errors/empty output, compiles in a private directory, and atomically renames the
   new data.cdb only after compilation succeeds. It publishes one source per run.

9. Before exposure: run both test suites; run `php bin/admin.php check-export`;
   compile its output with the actual deployed tinydns-data in an isolated directory;
   test two tenants, a group admin and senior admin, reset delivery, logout/revocation,
   AXFR/query allowlists and failure limits. Verify /src/config.php, /bin/admin.php,
   /tests/router.php, /security-review/, /.git/config, /axfr_get.php and
   /index.php/anything are unavailable. Verify the HTTPS cookie flags and headers,
   and that failed downloads/compiler failures leave the served CDB unchanged.
